\section{Make One Interface Own the Collector Boundary}

The collector should not know how to dispatch an opcode, and
the interpreter should not know how to decode forwarding
headers. Start with a small contract: allocate an object
using a descriptor and extent, enumerate and update all root
slots, enumerate pointer fields in each object, and perform
reference stores with the barriers the selected algorithm
requires. The Scheme implementation notes illustrate the
use of tagged value registers and an evaluation stack with
recognizable roots. Appel describes the corresponding
compiler contract using machine-level maps
\cite{appel2004modern}. These two root providers can coexist
in a runtime where a bytecode frame calls generated code,
which calls a native helper and eventually returns to bytecode.

For the interpreter, make the frame's published stack height
match the slots that still hold live values. Popping two
operands and decreasing the height before an allocating
helper call may hide both operands. Keeping all capacity
live forever instead retains old values and may read
uninitialized entries. In a precise tagged stack, publish
exactly the live interval and ensure a temporary root frame
covers any live values outside it. For compiled code, a map
and unwinder locate those slots; the same allocation primitive
can be used once the compiler arranges its live values in the
expected safe state.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Producer & Exposes & Pitfall \\
\hline
Interpreter & Live tagged stack slots & Popped operands across allocation \\
Generated code & Final stack maps and unwind data & Stale spill or register location \\
Native helper & Scoped handles & Raw pointer cached across reentry \\
Object layout & Descriptor-guided fields & Numeric slots traced as addresses \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\Cref{fig:visual-418} shows the shared interface. A slot
visitor implementing moving collection updates actual stack,
frame, or handle storage. The same path can implement a
nonmoving tracer whose visitor only reads. Keeping both
collectors behind the slot interface is useful for tests:
the nonmoving version can tolerate bugs in pointer repair,
while agreement on ordinary reachability is still informative.

