% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Walk an Allocating Bytecode Handler}

\begin{verbatim}
execute_make_pair(vm):
    # Entering this scope and reading slots cannot collect.
    with vm.temporary_roots(3) as tmp:
        tmp[0] := vm.peek(1)
        tmp[1] := vm.peek(0)
        tmp[2] := null
        vm.pop_n(2)
        tmp[2] := vm.allocate_pair_with_null_fields()
        # No collection between allocation return and root store.
        vm.write_field_through_handles(&tmp[2], left, &tmp[0])
        vm.write_field_through_handles(&tmp[2], right, &tmp[1])
        vm.push_root_value_without_collection(tmp[2])
\end{verbatim}

Reserve operand stack capacity and temporary roots before this
sequence, or expose a safe preparatory state while expanding
them. The allocation may copy both operands, so the field
initializers use the updated handle slots rather than saved
pointer values. If a barrier slow path may itself collect,
it needs a stable handle to the holder and must recompute
the field location after moving it. The sketch assumes the
allocation result is a parseable pair and that the final
push does not collect. If the allocation can throw, define
whether old operands are needed for exception transfer,
and put the interpreter in the corresponding stack state.
The temporary-root scope must unwind on either normal or
exceptional exit.

In generated code, specialize the analogous fast path only
after establishing the same invariants. A reserve check can
fall through to a noncollecting initialization block or branch
to an allocating runtime call. In the slow branch, the
compiler emits a map for the live operands; the call can
then change their addresses, so later machine uses must be
connected to the updated values. A map by itself is not a
dataflow edge in the optimizer. Model relocation explicitly
or use an ABI that makes the updated values available through
known slots. A common mistake is to load a raw pointer
before a call, map its original slot correctly, and continue
using the redundant, stale register afterward.

\section{Use an Independent Graph Oracle}

Generate bounded sequences of allocations, field writes,
root additions, and root removals. Use stable IDs in a
separate abstract graph and compute strong reachability
there, independently of the runtime's descriptor walker.
For a full collection with no finalizers or weak edges,
the graph reachable after the pause must be isomorphic to
the model: same IDs, field values, sharing, and cycles.
If testing generational behavior, compare reachable objects
and incoming-edge coverage separately from reclaimed bytes.
A young object retained by unreachable old storage may be
legal until the next full cycle. Repeat with weak and
conditional semantics only once the oracle models them.

Add directed fixtures to complement random operations.
Force collection in argument evaluation, at allocation
refill, during an exception, and across a managed-to-native
callback. Run at tiny heap sizes and trigger a move at every
eligible allocation. Disable inline allocation in one run to
exercise more slow paths, then enable it to verify the
compiler's fast-path assumptions. Poison abandoned memory or
protect an old range in debug builds so stale references
fail at use, and ensure the graph oracle does not keep
every expected object alive by storing managed references
to them in the test harness.

\section{Separate Allocation Failure From Graph Failure}

Inject failures in heap growth, worklist reservation, and
code metadata allocation. For each failure establish what
state is still intact and which subsystem may retry. An
evacuating collector cannot report an ordinary allocation
failure halfway through overwriting old headers unless its
fallback understands that transient state. By contrast,
failure to allocate a descriptor table before publishing any
objects can be handled without touching an existing heap.
Check that an exception does not skip temporary root cleanup
or leave a stopped mutator state permanently advertised.

Publish useful counters before tuning: allocation volume
and refill count, live bytes at collection, pause and
handshake distributions, scanned slots, and remembered
work if present. A regression with an increasing number
of scanned roots but stable live bytes suggests a different
investigation from one that copies an increasing live graph.
Keep a minimal replay for every discovered bug and record
the chosen collector and stress flags. Once the failure is
reproducible with stable IDs, it can often be localized to
one missing slot, missed barrier, or invalid layout rather
than being labeled a generic memory-management crash.

\section{Roll Out One New Invariant at a Time}

Start with nonmoving precise tracing and verify object fields
and root census before relying on relocation. Add copying
with a small explicit root interface, then introduce compiled
frames or a generational barrier separately. Keep a way to
select the baseline in debug runs. If a native transition only
fails after enabling the moving collector, inspect whether
its handles are rewritten and reloaded; if it also fails
under nonmoving tracing, inspect whether they are roots at
all. This difference turns two implementations into a
diagnostic tool rather than merely duplicate code.

Test transitions under both code aging and reentry. Arrange
for a generated frame to wait in a native callback that
invalidates its compiled entry point, then allocate in
the callback's interpreter frame. The census must include
the old generated frame using its old maps, along with the
native handles and the currently running bytecode frame.
On return the renewed entry point is unrelated to the
suspended invocation. Include a variant in which the native
helper saves only a derived address. It should either be
rejected by the interface or use a pin or a base handle with
an offset, not happen to pass because the test did not move
the object. These mixed-frame fixtures exercise a contract
that no isolated collector or backend test establishes.

Make the debug verifier check these fixtures immediately
before and after resumption. A proof that the heap is
parseable does not prove that the resumed function will reload
the repaired register rather than a stale cached copy.
